					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dankaar" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1790336); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1790336&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973932" alt="dankaar" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1790336)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dankaar" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1790336); return false;" >dankaar</a></span> <span class="odate time_1431006854 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">07 May 2015 13:54</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2287906">
									

<blockquote>
<p>I don't know if someone has yet come up with a plausible, alternate explanation to a monthly termination.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The problem is that monthly termination isn't all that plausible either. The US has approx. 3,000 death-row inmates in October of 2013. The appeals process is typically 15 years before the execution is carried out, and only 39 people were executed in 2013. Europe has largely abolished the Death Penalty.</p>
<p>Basically, there is not enough death row inmates worldwide to justify monthly terminations AND keep it secret; one or more people are going to find out that they keep disappearing, especially in the US. The only way it could be plausable is if, and only if, the D-class program is a miniscule part of the Foundation's testing program OR the Foundation is essentially run out of someone's garage.</p>
<p>It could be mitigated if the Foundation included political prisoners into the D-class program, but that has other ethical issues attached to it. Furthermore the countries most likely to allow the Foundation to take their prisoners are likely to have human rights violations in the first place and put under scrutiny by the UN whenever possible.</p>

							</div>

								
