														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2288004">
									

<p>All good points, although for the purposes of the Foundation-verse, I'd like to make a few myself:</p>
<p>-If their world is based on our own, then the 3,000 could just be &quot;the ones that we know of&quot;, with most of the rest removed from records and public consciousness (I would assume the Foundation would only recruit ones that didn't make the national media, or they suppress coverage of &quot;open-and-shut-case&quot; murders and then amnesticize everyone after the fact).</p>
<p>-Hey, the USA has the highest (recorded!) prison population per capita of any industrialized nation IRL. This could be fun to work into the SCP-verse in a narrative way&#8230; Maybe other countries are just as murdery as the US, but give more of their prison population to the Foundation for whatever reason. Heck, maybe the large US death row population is just where the Foundation keeps a &quot;strategic D-Class reserve&quot; just in case they need a bunch all at once. Perhaps they even do this so they <em>don't</em> have to use political prisoners except when absolutely necessary&#8230; Which I'm sure the Foundation would do in a pinch (if we're sticking with the &quot;cold, not cruel, and ethical when not prohibitively expensive&quot; Foundation) but would find distasteful.</p>
<p>Edit - One more:<br />
-Also, perhaps the Foundation only leaves two types of death row inmates on death row: unsure if they're actually guilty (presumably they review the case files) and so prefer to leave them to their appeal processes, and/or too famous of a case to conveniently cover up. Maybe the Foundation even has a standing agreement with many countries that if the evidence is strong enough against a person (standing over a bunch of bodies screaming &quot;HAHA I LOVE MURDER WOO!&quot; might suffice), they just memory-wipe everyone without a trial and summarily D-Class-ify the person to save everyone the trouble and to avoid the murderer getting media coverage, which somewhat hinders the Foundation's goal of putting them in an orange jumpsuit and putting them on Keter duty.</p>

							</div>

								
