		<div class="post" id="post-2288844">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2288844)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2288844">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kholettedrake" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1909832); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1909832&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973932" alt="KholetteDrake" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1909832)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kholettedrake" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1909832); return false;" >KholetteDrake</a></span> <span class="odate time_1431106768 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">08 May 2015 17:39</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2288844">
									

<p>While it is explicitly stated that most D-Class come from the ranks of Death Row inmates, I have to imagine that policy was created when the numbers of SCP objects in containment was still fairly small and there were enough prisoners to support such a policy. Nowadays, with the vast increase in objects in containment, as mentioned above, the Foundation would be going through a minimum of 25,000 prisoners per year. That's the population of a small town.</p>
<p>The Foundation (in its current incarnation) has existed roughly since around WWI. My back of the napkin calculations (I am neither mathematician nor statistician, so someone else can probably generate better numbers) using the number of adults in US prisons as of 2011, the Foundation would have run through the entire prison population right around the year 2000. It's just not practicable to exterminate so many D-Class so frequently. I would have to think the Foundation has adapted this practice, keeping more D-Class around, especially those who cooperate or adapt well to working in the Foundation.</p>
<p>However, there does exist a population of people, largely ignored and untracked in any significant way that crosses all national, ethnic and religious boundaries. The homeless. With roughly 100 million individuals worldwide, and more joining their ranks all the time, I would think that to the Foundation, they look like a perfect source of D-Class personnel, especially if the policy of monthly disposal has been shifted or adapted. In fact, if this tale were the canon way the Foundation was now disposing of D-Class, using the homeless as research fodder would actually be more humane than using prisoners. For a month of Keter-duty, an individual gets a new life and all they have to do is survive and then be a sleeper spy. This sounds exactly like something the Foundation would do. In my headcanon, at least.</p>

							</div>

								
					
